<section>
  <div class="container llms-explainer">

    <h2>How the Matching Works</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>Word overlap with the URL and title.</strong> Each keyword is compared word by word with the words in a page's URL and, if you add one, its title. Stop words are ignored and simple plurals are treated as the same word.</li>
      <li><strong>Three outcomes.</strong> A strong match is mapped, a partial match is marked for review, and no match means the keyword probably needs a new page.</li>
      <li><strong>Near-duplicate keywords.</strong> Keywords made of the same words in a different order, such as "seo for restaurants" and "restaurant seo", are grouped so you can target them with one page.</li>
      <li><strong>Pages with no keyword.</strong> Any URL that no keyword matched is listed, which often points to thin pages or pages that could be merged.</li>
      <li><strong>Suggested slugs.</strong> For keywords that need a page, the tool proposes a clean URL slug.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What a Keyword Map Is For</h2>
    <p>A keyword map links each search term you want to win to one page that should answer it. It prevents two common problems: leaving good keywords without a page, and publishing several pages that chase the same search. Neither is a Google rule; it is a planning habit that keeps a site tidy as it grows, and it is easiest to do before you write rather than after.</p>
    <p>This tool does the first pass using words alone, so it cannot judge search intent. A keyword like "plumber salary" shares a word with "plumber near me" but needs a different kind of page. Treat the results as a draft, check anything marked for review against what the page actually says, and use the grouped duplicates to decide which single page should own a topic.</p>

    <h2>Related Questions About Keyword Mapping</h2>
    <h3>Keyword Mapping Tool: What It Does</h3>
    <p>This <strong>keyword mapping tool</strong> matches each keyword to the page it fits best, flags keywords that need a new page, and warns about near-duplicate keywords that could compete. People also search for it as keywordmapping or keyword mapping software. It runs in your browser with no signup.</p>
    <h3>Keyword Mapping Generator: How the Matching Works</h3>
    <p>Each keyword is compared word by word with the words in a page's URL and its title, if you add one. Stop words are ignored and simple plurals count as the same word. A strong match is mapped, a partial match is marked for review, and no match means the keyword probably needs a new page, for which the tool proposes a clean slug.</p>
    <h3>Keyword Map Tool: Near-Duplicate Keywords</h3>
    <p>Keywords made of the same words in a different order, such as seo for restaurants and restaurant seo, are grouped so you can target them with one page. Any URL that no keyword matched is listed too, which often points to thin pages or pages that could be merged.</p>
    <h3>Keyword Mapper: What a Keyword Map Is For</h3>
    <p>A keyword map links each search term you want to win to one page that should answer it. It prevents two problems: leaving good keywords without a page, and publishing several pages that chase the same search. Neither is a Google rule, it is a planning habit that is easiest before you write.</p>
    <h3>SEO Keyword Mapping Tool: What It Cannot Judge</h3>
    <p>It matches on words alone, so it cannot judge search intent. A keyword like plumber salary shares a word with plumber near me but needs a different kind of page. Treat the result as a draft and check anything marked for review against what the page says. See <a href="/blog/content-gap-analysis">content gap analysis</a> for the next step.</p>
    <h3>Keyword Mapping Tool Free: Add Volume and Export</h3>
    <p>It is a free keyword mapping tool with no limit of its own, and a few hundred keywords and pages work comfortably. Add search volume after a comma and it appears in the table and the CSV, with the biggest keywords first inside each group. Group related terms first with the <a href="/tools/keyword-grouping-tool">keyword grouping tool</a>, or choose research sources in our <a href="/blog/keyword-research-tools">keyword research tools</a> guide.</p>
    <h2>Keyword Mapping Tool FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Does the tool check what is on my pages?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. It matches keywords to the words in each URL and optional title you provide. It does not fetch your pages, so it cannot see the content or the search intent.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Should every keyword get its own page?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Close variations of the same search, like "seo for restaurants" and "restaurant seo", usually belong on one page. The tool groups those so you can pick a single owner page.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What does "needs a new page" mean?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No existing URL shares enough words with the keyword. It may mean a real content gap, or that your URLs are named differently from how people search, so check before creating anything.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I include search volume?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. Add a comma and a number after a keyword and it appears in the table and the CSV, and keywords are listed with the biggest first inside each group.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How many keywords can I paste?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>The tool has no limit of its own, but very long lists may feel slow in the browser. A few hundred keywords and pages work comfortably.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my data anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Everything is matched in your browser with JavaScript, nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is there a free keyword mapping tool?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. This one is free, needs no signup and maps your keywords to your pages in your browser, with a CSV export.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does keyword mapping check search intent?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. This tool matches keywords to the words in your URLs and titles only, so check intent yourself before you create or merge a page.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

